WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.570
Your event plan, your offer, and your first 90 days.

2
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:11.600
This is the video for module four of Catering Basics and Food Safety: your event plan, your offer and your next steps.

3
00:00:12.850 --> 00:00:22.940
Your capstone is one complete plan for a real or realistic event. It should be good enough that another trained cook could follow it and deliver the job safely.

4
00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:26.370
You will add a written food safety plan.

5
00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:31.240
You will learn to present your service with specific, honest proof.

6
00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:34.560
And you will plan your next 90 days.

7
00:00:35.810 --> 00:00:43.410
Your plan has six parts. Client and event details. The menu with portion sizes. The quantity and shopping sheet.

8
00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:53.700
Your costing and quotation. Your production timetable. And a food safety plan with logs. You already have most of these from earlier lessons.

9
00:00:54.950 --> 00:01:04.150
Professional kitchens use a system called HACCP. Its thinking is simple. For each step, ask what could go wrong, what stops it, and how you will check.

10
00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:17.960
Funmi plans 9 kilograms of coleslaw for 100 guests. Coleslaw is never cooked, so every step matters. She buys firm, fresh vegetables and salad cream with an intact seal and a valid date.

11
00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:25.780
She washes the vegetables in safe water, uses the green ready-to-eat board, and washes her hands first.

12
00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:32.940
The shredded vegetables go into covered containers in the fridge, below 5 degrees, overnight.

13
00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:39.540
She adds the dressing on the morning of the event, in a sanitised bowl, and keeps it chilled.

14
00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:54.450
It travels in a separate cooler with ice packs. She records 4 degrees at packing, and checks again on arrival. Write clear steps a helper can follow: check chicken at 70 degrees is clearer than cook properly.

15
00:01:55.700 --> 00:02:00.950
Clients hear my food is delicious from every caterer. Vague words do not win bookings.

16
00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:14.510
Specific details do, because a client can check them. Use only photos of your own food, in daylight, and testimonials with permission. Never use internet photos of other caterers' food.

17
00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:23.950
Ngozi replies within the hour. She thanks the client and asks for the date, venue, serving time, format and any allergies.

18
00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:35.930
Package A is jollof, chicken, moi moi and coleslaw, at about 4,200 naira per head. For 70 guests that is 294,000 naira.

19
00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:46.670
Package B is fried rice, turkey, plantain and salad, at about 5,500 per head, or 385,000 naira.

20
00:02:47.920 --> 00:03:01.150
She adds that food is held above 60 degrees in insulated coolers and that they arrive at least one hour before serving. She offers a paid tasting and states the 50% deposit to confirm the date.

21
00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:14.840
Amaka finishes the course with no paid jobs yet. In days 1 to 30, she does two test cooks for family events, buys a probe thermometer and a second cooler, and finalises her offer.

22
00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:27.990
In days 31 to 60, she shares her offer on WhatsApp Status and with her church group, and aims for two paid jobs of 30 to 50 guests, each with a 50% deposit.

23
00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:40.500
In days 61 to 90, she reviews leftovers, cost accuracy and timing, adjusts portions and prices, and decides whether to accept jobs up to 80 guests.

24
00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:55.210
Keep business money in a separate account or wallet. When you grow, ask CAC about registering a business name, NAFDAC if you package food for shops, and your local environmental health office about permits.

25
00:03:56.460 --> 00:04:06.210
To remember. Six parts make one complete plan. For every step, name the hazard, the control and the check. Specific, honest offers win bookings.

26
00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:16.760
Cook to 70 degrees, hold above 60 or below 5, and never more than 2 hours at room temperature. Then test, deliver small, review and grow.

27
00:04:18.010 --> 00:04:31.600
Now practise. Draft hazard and control tables for your two highest-risk dishes, and do a small test cook with a temperature log. Create your one-page offer with your own food photo. And write your 90-day plan.

28
00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:33.890
Continue with the lessons below.
